{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Toulouse and ruled the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and succeeded his father as count at the age of 12. During his early years as count Raymond III was under the regency of his mother who played a significant role in the government of the county.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He succeeded his father as count in 1152 and was under the regency of his mother who played a significant role in the government of the county. During this period the county was facing threats from the Zengid dynasty and the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\n*The Zengid Threat*\n\nIn 1153 the Zengid dynasty under Nur ad-Din Zangi captured the city of Damascus and began to threaten the County of Tripoli. Raymond III formed an alliance with the Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to counter the Zengid threat. In 1163 the Zengids launched a major attack on the county but were defeated at the Battle of al-Buqaia. Raymond III also formed an alliance with the Byzantine Empire and received military support from Emperor Manuel I Komnenos.\n\n*The Third Crusade*\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined the forces of King Guy of Lusignan and Conrad of Montferrat to defend the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin. The combined forces were defeated at the Battle of Hattin and Raymond III was taken prisoner. He was later released and returned to the County of Tripoli where he died in 1187.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.35264554619789124}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logpr